Expanded story:
Amy hovered over the sizzling skillet, her hands moving rhythmically as she folded beaten eggs into fluffy batter for Will’s pancakes and amber‑gold waffles beside him. The kitchen hummed with morning light, the hum of the fridge punctuating their giggles.

A sudden crash rang from the front door—an unsteady slam that rattled the windows. A zombie burst through the frame, its jaw slackened, eyes fixed on the two children in her arms. Amy’s heart leapt; she yanked Will and Amber onto her hip and shoved them down the narrow stairs into the basement, slamming the heavy hatch shut behind them.

She dropped them onto a rickety chair at the back of the dim room and stepped back onto the kitchen floor. With one swift motion she pushed aside the refrigerator door to reveal a pistol in its back panel and a steel katana tucked beside it. She grabbed both weapons, her fingers trembling with adrenaline.

The first zombie staggered from the living room doorway, claws clattering on hardwood. Amy fired the pistol at its head; the bullet burst through bone and flesh, sending the creature sprawling. A second lunged, but she swung the katana in a wide arc, slicing it cleanly across the midsection before it hit the floor with a thud.

More zombies surged from every cracked window: one pushed past the kitchen counter, another clawing its way into the hallway. Amy moved between them, firing shot after headshot and slashing with precise strokes; each zombie fell to the ground in a cascade of blood and broken limbs.

When the last zombie collapsed beside her, its eyes still glazed, she breathed out a shaky sigh. The basement was silent except for the echoing thud of her own pulse against the floorboards.

She turned back toward the kitchen doorway, scooped up Will and Amber with arms wrapped around them, and pushed through the hatch into the bright sunlight again. Together they stepped onto the front porch, Amy’s shoulders squared as she held the pistol and katana at the ready for whatever came next.
